edb/ref 1061 120074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05ea8d4a-447c-5dfe-bc07-9429c6506a99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152884 #edb/ref 1061 120077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b41146a-7652-5f80-8f71-bd202ae02ffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152885 #edb/ref 1061 120080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88af8584-370e-538d-bc31-ffbea331ec2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152886 #edb/ref 1061 120083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42a89cf9-ea00-5196-9579-5a0eda504d5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152887 #edb/ref 1061 120084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ca4d75a-1805-5443-b3f6-3a98bafec5cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153426 #edb/ref 1064 "The **negation** of a mathematical statement is its *opposite* statement. A mathematical statement and its negation have opposite truth values.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The negation of the *true* statement \"$4$ is divisible by $2$\" is the *false* statement \"$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\"\n- The negation of the *false* statement \"$5$ is even\" is the *true* statement \"$5$ is odd.\"\n\nWe can express negations using the symbol \"$\\neg$\". The negation of $A$ is denoted $\\neg A,$ and has the opposite truth values as $A{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nWe can negate compound statements as well. For example, the statement $\\neg (A \\land B)$ has the opposite truth values as the statement $A \\land B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153426 #edb/ref 1065 7965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153426 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65ae07d1-2d1e-5f50-9c5f-4b45ca435aed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153426 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153427 #edb/ref 1064 "When we have two negations in a row, we can cancel them out.\n\n- For example, $\\neg (\\neg A)$ can be simplified to $A.$\n- Likewise, $\\neg (\\neg (A \\land B))$ can be simplified to $A \\land B.$\n\nTo understand why negations cancel with each other, consider the following statement:\n\n$4$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\n\nIf we negate again, we get back the original sentence:\n\n$4$ divisible by $2.$\n\nSo, if we negate a statement twice, we end up with the original statement.\n\nMore formally, we can prove that $\\neg (\\neg A) \\equiv A$ using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg(\\neg A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nSince $A$ and $\\neg(\\neg A)$ have the same truth values, they are logically equivalent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153427 #edb/ref 1065 7966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153427 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "96c8056f-c1d0-5453-ba7f-71700a48506c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153427 #edb/ref 1067 "Double Negations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-176858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$-1$ is greater than $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWhat is the negation of this statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c30bbae-c196-5bdb-9a48-5e29a02258c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374366 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the given statement as $P.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is not true that $-1$ is greater than $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIn other words, $-1$ is *not* greater than $-\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$-1$ is **not** greater than $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374367 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea3ba7f3-2de5-5652-942e-f697424cd57a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374368 #edb/ref 1080 "$-1$ is not greater than $-\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374368 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17